Organization = {INT ASSOC HYDRAUL RES; INT ASSOC WATER QUAL; ENVIRONM CANADA; ENVIRONM
   ONTARIO; AMER SOC CIVIL ENGINEERS; GOVT CANADA, GREAT LAKE CLEANUP FUND},
Abstract = {This study aims to evaluate the applicability of satellite imagery in
   estimating the percentage of impervious area in urbanized areas. Two
   methods of estimation are proposed and applied to a small urbanized
   watershed in Japan. The area is considered under two different cases of
   subdivision; i.e., 14 zones and 17 zones. The satellite imageries of
   LANDSAT-MSS (Multi-Spectral Scanner) in 1984, MOS-MESSR(Multi-spectral
   Electronic Self-Scanning Radiometer) in 1988 and SPOT-HRV(High
   Resolution Visible) in 1988 are classified. The percentage of
   imperviousness in 17 zones is estimated by using these classification
   results. These values are compared with the ones obtained from the
   aerial photographs. The percent imperviousness derived from the imagery
   agrees well with those derived from aerial photographs. The estimation
   errors evaluated are less than 10\%, the same as those obtained from
   aerial photographs.},
