   WREP 2014},
Year = {2014},
Pages = {1-8},
Note = {International Conference on Water Resource and Environmental Protection
   (WREP), Hong Kong, PEOPLES R CHINA, JUN 07-08, 2014},
Abstract = {This research is dedicated to the operation with microbial fuel cell
   (MFC) thus microorganisms on the anode oxidize substrates it generates
   electrons, protons, and other metabolic products while the potential
   cathode surface reduces terminal electron acceptor with received protons
   and electrons. The characteristics of MFCs were widely studied at 20
   degrees C. There is comparative analysis of unicameral (MFC A) and
   bicameral (MFC B) reactors at 15 degrees C in this research. The MFC A
   had peak voltage at 320 mV to over 525 mV and was maintained at around
   500 mV over the complete cycle 5-6 d. Conversely, the MFC B peak value
   of 620 mV, slowly dropped to 470 mV on 9 d, and then rapidly to <100 mV
   on 10 d. Electrochemical analysis revealed that the MFC B had lower
   internal resistance than MFC A, with the former having much lower anodic
   resistance than the latter. Microbial analysis showed that the
   Betaproteobacteria concentration was much higher in clone accounts for
   MFC B then in the MFC A. This study shows that more simple and compact
   configurations such as one-chamber system are required for reducing
   construction and operation cost.},
