Note = {International Conference on Water Resource and Environmental Protection
   (WREP), Hong Kong, PEOPLES R CHINA, JUN 07-08, 2014},
Abstract = {On the basis of analysis on main problems in China's water resource
   management, this paper intends to propose a strategic conception of
   national smart water grid project as a future water resource management
   platform, and introduces concept and system structure of smart water
   grid (SWG), and construction idea of intelligent physical, information
   and scheduling networks. Moreover, it analyzes realization approaches
   and remarkable advantages of water safety strategy supported by national
   SWG project and recent main tasks to promote national smart SWG project
   construction. Finally, relevant suggestions on national SWG project
   construction are set forth.
   The smart water grid (SWG) is a new concept emerging in the water
   management in recent years. It is an important presentation of
   transformation of water management to which modern information with
   ``intelligence{''} is applied. Since IBM first set forth the concept
   smart planet in 2008, numerous new and high-tech industrial companies
   such as IBM, Hitachi and Schneider intended to interpret the idea of
   smart water management and layout of business, and developed water
   information technologies such as perception and mining of
   high-performance information and efficient automation control.
   Meanwhile, such countries as US, Australia and Israel launched their
   national water grid strategy respectively through a nearly 20-year
   exploration, taking it as an important platform to cope with problems of
   global climate change and complicated water resources{[}1]. In order to
   address increasingly severe and complicated water resource situation,
   Chinese researchers are also making energetic effort to explore a new
   path to the SWG with Chinese characteristics. Depending on modern
   information technology and advanced water management, they are carrying
   forward the transformation of the advanced multi-objective water
   management from the traditional discrete and single objective.},
