Note = {13th International Conference on Intellectual Capital Knowledge
   Management and Organisational Learning (ICICKM), Ithaca Coll, Ithaca,
   NY, OCT 14-15, 2016},
Organization = {Marist Coll},
Abstract = {Purpose In the era of globalization, it has become clear that leaders
   are those who should drive the process of studying and be maximally
   effective in a global knowledge environment. Knowledge and learning play
   a key role in success and competitiveness, especially in education. The
   purpose of this paper is to investigate the processes of Knowledge
   Management and Leadership, to give their definitions, to explore their
   relationship, and to explain how Knowledge Management and Leadership can
   help both students and teachers improve their outcomes. Design,
   methodology, approach Data for this study of Knowledge Management (KM)
   and Leadership (L) combinations comes from a literature review, student
   essays, and online questionnaire. The question of Knowledge Management
   and Leadership was considered in the works of such scientists as
   Petrides, Nodine, Drucker, Mahce, Covey, Bush, and others that establish
   the theoretical significance of the work. In addition, we gathered and
   analyzed short essays on the subject from 10 students. Finally, we
   gathered data about students' knowledge and perceptions from an online
   questionnaire. Structure: The paper is structured into two parts: the
   first part is theoretical, devoted to the analysis of KM and L
   approaches; the second part is an empirical study. In the conclusion, we
   summarize the findings and propose future research directions. Practical
   implications: This paper has implications for educational leaders at
   schools and universities combining their KM strategies and L systems. It
   can also be helpful for students studying aspects of KM and Educational
   Leadership (EL). Originality/value: The originality of the paper is that
   it provides an empirical study of main characteristics of ``a leader{''}
   for Russian students. In our paper, we also highlighted the importance
   of ``Knowledge Management{''} because about 40 \% of the interviewed
   still do not know the subject.},
