   Sciences and Arts (SGEM 2014), Albena, BULGARIA, SEP 01-10, 2014},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {Youth unemployment has been recognised by the EC as one of the most
   serious issues to be tackled in the upcoming programming period {[}1]
   for its extremely high levels across the EU-28 resulting in 5.4 million
   jobless Europeans aged bellow 25. The number of young people that are
   not employed, not in education and not in training (NEETs) is even
   higher, i.e. 7.5 million in age between 15 and 24. Two key risk factors
   have been identified for becoming a NEET disadvantage and disengagement
   {[}2]. These occur more often among youth from families suffering from
   poverty, with unemployed parents, disabled youth or youth with ethnic
   origins.
   Despite featuring different youth unemployment and NEET rates {[}2], the
   two countries chosen for this study Bulgaria and the Czech Republic are
   both home to quite large numbers of young people with Romany origin that
   live more often than other groups of young people in the above mentioned
   disadvantaged conditions. The central issue of the proposed paper is to
   present the views of young Romany people on the education, employment
   and their future life prospects. The undertaken research investigates
   what problems and barriers young Roma encounter in education and the
   labour market entry and what are their strategies for transition from
   school to work. The role of public policies in supporting young Roma
   will be assessed in the light of their perceptions and strategies.
   The research is based on 90 interviews and 5 focus groups organised with
   Roma youth in age from 16 to 30. Data collection took place in the
   selected deprived neighbourhoods in Sofia (Bulgaria) and Brno (Czech
   Republic) in spring 2014. This research has been carried out within the
   CITISPYCE project funded by the FP7of the European Union for Research
   and Technology Development.},
